5:[["$","$L10","1.6",{"topic":{"id":"1.6","title":"Overview of Information Technology Act and Cyber Laws","minutes":16,"summary":"Navigate the IT Act by function: recognition, security, offences, interception, blocking, intermediary liability, and adjudication.","sections":[{"title":"Recognition and security provisions","body":["Chapter II and related provisions recognize electronic governance, legal recognition of electronic records, and electronic signatures. Sections 4–10A address areas such as writing, signature, retention, and electronic contracts, subject to exclusions. Recognition does not dispense with proof of authenticity or compliance with other laws. Electronic signatures and Digital Signature Certificates are related but not identical to every form of scanned signature.","Section 43 addresses specified unauthorized acts causing damage and compensation; section 66 criminalizes dishonest or fraudulent commission of acts referred to in section 43. Sections 65 and 65B of the IT Act concern source-code matters and must not be confused with the old Evidence Act section 65B. Sections 66C and 66D address identity theft and cheating by personation."]},{"title":"State powers and intermediary safeguards","body":["Section 69 concerns interception, monitoring, or decryption under prescribed conditions; section 69A concerns blocking public access in the interests specified by the statute; section 69B concerns specified traffic-data collection. These are different powers. BNSS section 94 provides the general power to require production of necessary documents or things, including electronic records. A production order is not automatically authority for a full device search.","Section 79 provides conditional intermediary safe harbour. Its operation must be read with section 79(2)–(3), the applicable Rules, and judicial decisions. Section 80 preserves specified police entry, search, and arrest powers for offences under the Act, subject to its conditions. The existence of a power does not remove constitutional, procedural, or documentation requirements."]},{"title":"Institutions and routes to remedy","body":["CERT-In under section 70B is the national incident-response agency for specified functions. Section 70A previously designated NCIIPC functions concerning critical information infrastructure; institutional titles and notifications should be checked. The Controller and certifying authorities support electronic-signature infrastructure. Adjudication and appellate mechanisms have changed over time, so do not rely uncritically on old descriptions of a separate Cyber Appellate Tribunal.","A cyber incident may involve a police complaint, a civil claim, a regulatory report, a platform grievance, and preservation requests in parallel. Map the remedy to the objective: stop loss, preserve evidence, remove unlawful content, obtain compensation, or prosecute an offender. Keep originals and a record of what each recipient was asked to do."]}],"practice":{"title":"Choose the right power","scenario":"An investigator needs subscriber records from a service provider and also wants to block a public fraudulent page.","steps":["Separate the records request from the public-access restriction.","Locate BNSS section 94 and IT Act section 69A and read their conditions.","Identify who can authorize each action and what must be recorded.","Add evidence preservation so blocking does not become accidental destruction."],"debrief":"A precise power-and-purpose map prevents the common error of treating all cyber investigative actions as one general permission."},"terms":[],"lawIds":["it-act","it-2021","bnss"],"sources":["itact","shreya","cert","rules"],"labId":"$undefined","unit":1,"objectives":["Explain recognition and security provisions and apply it to an investigation.","Explain state powers and intermediary safeguards and apply it to an investigation.","Explain institutions and routes to remedy and apply it to an investigation."],"question":{"id":"q-1.6","prompt":"Which IT Act provision concerns blocking public access to information?","options":["Section 4","Section 43A","Section 69A","Section 66C"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Section 69A addresses blocking for the specified statutory interests, following the applicable procedure.","unit":1}}}]]
